  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Chances for thunderstorms this afternoon for north-central\n   Idaho, and west-central and southwest Montana. About 10% chance\n   for storms to become severe.\n\n-  Shower and thunderstorm activity will continue each afternoon \n   through early next week.\n\n-  Warming and drying latter part of next week under a ridge of\n   high pressure.\n\n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft will bring moisture into the Northern\nRockies this afternoon. The moisture feed will coincide with a\nshortwave disturbance moving southwest to northeast over north-\ncentral Idaho into western Montana this afternoon and evening.\nModel soundings indicate enough instability to fuel thunderstorm\ndevelopment in the aforementioned areas. High resolution models\nshow storms tracking with the shortwave, southwest to northeast\nalong approximately U.S. Highway 12. Primary threats from\nthunderstorms will be small hail, gusty outflow winds of about\n40-50 mph, heavy rain, and frequent lightning. There is about a 10\npercent chance that storms become severe, meaning one-inch\ndiameter hail and gusts of 50-60 mph.  \n\nUpper level flow transitions to westerly on Sunday as a broad\nridge of high pressure builds over the western U.S. The moisture\nfeed stays uninterrupted, though, and will continue to support \nsome thunderstorm activity in the afternoon. As next week\nprogresses, a trough of low pressure amplifying over the eastern\nPacific will bring southwesterly flow aloft to the Northern\nRockies. This setup will keep afternoon thunderstorms in the\nforecast for the first part of the work week. High pressure\ncoinciding with a much drier airmass will move back in for the \nlatter part of the week.",
